{"groupId":"CHK-007","requirement":"[G7.5] Benefit versus cost — fair value. Limb: Consistency. Does the suitability report disclose all material charges relevant to the recommendation, including applicable initial, ongoing, product, platform, investment and transaction costs, and where higher costs arise, does the report clearly explain the benefits expected to be received by the client in exchange for those costs and are they supported by and consistent with the evidence provided?","reportSays":"The report asserts seven material benefits to justify the recommendation: (1) Lower overall cost structure compared to existing pension, meaning more savings remain invested for growth [P1, page 7]; (2) Lifestyling funds automatically reduce investment risk as approaching retirement, eventually moving portfolio outside agreed risk range [P1, page 7]; (3) Greater Investment Choice allowing more tailored strategy suited to risk profile with scope for future adjustments [P1, page 7]; (4) Adviser Charging Facilitation allowing direct deduction of charges from plan without separate payments [P1, page 7]; (5) Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments by adviser to ensure alignment with changing needs and market conditions [P1, page 7]; (6) Multi-asset fund selection providing professionally managed diversified investment solution aligned with financial goals and risk profile [P1, page 12]; (7) Enhanced Retirement Flexibility providing more flexible retirement options for tax-efficient withdrawal strategies [P1, page 7]. The report states recommended Aviva advised funds have demonstrated stronger performance compared to existing Aviva holdings [P1, page 13], and that Aviva platform funds align with agreed risk tolerance and have delivered comparable performance with reduced volatility [P1, page 13]. The report claims based on past performance of recommended funds, the plan is believed achievable to outperform by critical yield of 4.8% per year to age 75 [P1, page 11], stated as 'I do believe that this is achievable' [P1, page 13].","fileSays":"Evidence shows: (1) Fund recommendation table [P6] lists HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc at 50% with 0.18% annual charge and CT Universal MAP Balanced C at 50% with 0.29% annual charge. Research report [P3] confirms these funds' performance data. (2) Existing Aviva arrangement shows 0.50% fund charge [P1, page 5 table]. Performance comparison chart [P1, page 13] shows Aviva Risk 5 portfolio (B) achieved 37.25% over 3 years and 15.58% over 5 years as at 13/10/2025, versus existing Aviva Pension My Future Focus Consolidation (A) at 22.89% over 3 years and 15.58% over 5 years. (3) Zurich Managed Pn AP (D) achieved 34.56% over 3 years and 44.32% over 5 years, outperforming recommended Aviva Risk 5 portfolio (B) which achieved 37.25% over 3 years and 15.58% over 5 years. (4) Report states 'However, they have not outperformed your current Zurich funds' [P1, page 13]. (5) Volatility analysis [P1, page 13] shows Aviva Risk 5 portfolio (B) volatility of 7.56% versus Zurich Managed Pn AP (A) volatility of 7.89%. (6) Discrete performance table for HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio shows 2024 return of 10.82% and YTD 7.7% [P3, P5]. CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund shows 2024 return of 11.27% and YTD 8.04% [P3, P5]. (7) Fact Find [P10, P11] shows client net monthly income £1,430 against monthly expenditure £1,718, creating deficit of £288 [P14]. (8) Intelliflo proposed scenario [P23] lists People's Pension at £6,601, Scottish Widows at £211, Aviva at £3,744.36, Zurich at £103,439.24, Standard Life at £3,002. (9) Report page 5 table [P1] shows five existing arrangements totalling £116,998.47. Report page 9 shows recommended transfer value of £110,185. Shortfall of £6,813.47 is unexplained.","discrepancies":["Report asserts 'Lower Costs' as key benefit but existing Aviva charge is 0.50% and recommended Aviva Platform shows 0.44% combined charge (0.21% platform + 0.22% investment management per charge arithmetic). Text on page 6 states 'The plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' which contradicts the stated cost saving benefit.","Report claims recommended Aviva advised funds 'have demonstrated stronger performance compared to your existing Aviva holdings' [P1, page 13] but cumulative performance at 13/10/2025 shows Aviva Risk 5 portfolio (B) and existing Aviva Pension My Future Focus Consolidation (A) both at 15.58% over 5 years (identical, not stronger).","Report states recommended funds 'have not outperformed your current Zurich funds' [P1, page 13], yet recommends consolidating Zurich (£103,439.24) into lower-performing Aviva. Zurich achieved 44.32% over 5 years versus recommended Aviva at 15.58%.","Report claims benefit of outperformance at critical yield of 4.8% per year to age 75 [P1, page 11] as 'achievable' based on 'past performance of your recommended funds' but provides no performance data reconciling to this specific rate. Charge arithmetic implies fund value of £110,188.68 at 4.24% but no arrangement equals this amount.","Report transfer value shows £110,185 on page 9 but sum of five existing arrangements equals £116,998.47 (£6,813.47 shortfall unexplained).","Report page 5 table shows Zurich arrangement with 0.18% fund charge plus £2.12 per month admin, but charge arithmetic for EA5 and EA5 impact rows compute on Zurich Managed Pn AP (£103,438.46) not on SL at retirement universal pension (£3,002), creating unreliable comparison basis."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report's core claim of 'Lower Costs' as a benefit is contradicted by its own charge figures. The recommended Aviva platform combined charge of 0.44% is lower than the existing Aviva 0.50%, supporting a modest cost saving only for that arrangement. However, the consolidation includes Zurich at 0.93% (£961.98 on £103,438.71), Scottish Widows at 0.29%, and Aviva at 0.50%, with a blended existing charge substantially higher than the flat recommended 0.44%. The report's statement that 'The plan itself is 0.06% more expensive than the recommended solution' directly contradicts the stated benefit. The performance justification fails on two grounds: (1) the recommended Aviva Risk 5 funds matched (not exceeded) the existing Aviva Pension My Future Focus performance at 15.58% over 5 years, contradicting the claim of 'stronger performance'; (2) the Zurich Managed Pn AP at 44.32% over 5 years substantially outperformed both, yet consolidation into lower-performing Aviva is recommended. The report acknowledges this: 'However, they have not outperformed your current Zurich funds.' The critical yield assertion of 4.8% annually is stated as 'achievable' without supporting analysis. The passage cited [P1, page 13] contains only the statement 'Based on the past performance of your recommended funds, I do believe that this is achievable' — a claim unsupported by performance reconciliation to the 4.8% rate. Per the guard 'An assertion that required outperformance is achievable is a claim, not an analysis; require supporting performance data before accepting it.' The transfer value discrepancy (£110,185 recommended vs £116,998.47 sum of existing) is material and unexplained, suggesting either missing funds or undisclosed reductions. The charge arithmetic for EA5 rows uses Zurich Managed Pn AP fund value (£103,438.46) rather than the SL fund value (£3,002), making any comparison resting on those rows unreliable. The client's financial position [P14] shows monthly deficit of £288, yet the report does not address how ongoing adviser charging (implied by 'Adviser Charging Facilitation' benefit) will be funded from this negative cashflow. Vulnerability: The client is an HGV driver earning £300 per week net, with variable income noted as 'typically' [P1, page 3] not deterministic. This income variability is not reflected in the suitability analysis, and ongoing adviser fees on a volatile income base are not addressed."