A cost comparison table aggregating all charges (platform, investment management, fund charges, adviser fees, transaction costs) across existing arrangements and the recommended solution to support the 'Lower Costs' claim, as the canonical charge arithmetic shows inconsistent fund values across different charge layers.","Evidence of the specific benefits the client will receive from 'Adviser Charging Facilitation' and whether existing arrangements precluded this or charged differently, to distinguish between a new benefit and a procedural convenience."],"analysis":"The report identifies eight benefits to support the recommendation, but several fail the consistency test against evidence and the guards. First, 'Enhanced Retirement Flexibility' is already present in existing arrangements (Zurich offers full/partial UFPLS, Standard Life offers FAD per P5 and P9), so under the guard that a benefit the existing plan already provides cannot justify extra cost, this cannot be relied upon. Second, 'Greater Investment Choice' is marginal: Standard Life (300 funds) exceeds Aviva (80+ funds) substantially, so choice per se is not a material differentiator justifying cost. Third, the lifestyling risk benefit is internally inconsistent: the report cites lifestyling as a reason to switch, but the recommended funds are static risk-rated instruments, not a solution to the lifestyling problem — they replace one approach with another, and the same client is being moved to Aviva, which the evidence shows (P7) already employs lifestyling in the existing Aviva pension my future focus consolidation. The benefit claimed is addressing risk drift, not cost-justifying the switch. Fourth, the 'Lower Costs' claim is contradicted by arithmetic: the recommended solution is stated at 0.44% ongoing (page 11, P22: 0.21% platform + 0.22% investment management), but this is not lower than the existing Standard Life arrangement at 0.18% (canonical model, page 5), and the text on page 6 (P21) contradicts itself by stating 'the plan itself is 0.06% more expensive' when the comparison table implies it is cheaper. This internal contradiction and the fact that at least one existing arrangement is substantially lower undermines the lower-cost justification. Fifth, 'Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments' and 'Adviser Charging Facilitation' are claimed without supporting evidence of the adviser agreement, fee structure, or specific services that differentiate this from existing arrangements. The report does not evidence that existing advisers were not providing similar monitoring, or that the new structure delivers measurably more value. Sixth, the cost justification based on 'past performance' and critical yield (pages 11, 13, P21) relies on the assertion that recommended funds will 'outperform by 4.8% per year' to justify the 0.44% annual charge, but this is not supported by documented performance comparison between existing and recommended funds in the evidence, nor is the critical yield calculation shown. The guards state that a benefit the client will never use cannot support cost, and consolidation convenience is real but not financial; here, consolidation and monitoring benefits are presented as cost-justifiers without quantifying the financial impact or proving the client values them over lower-cost alternatives. The report's articulation of benefits reads as general value propositions for Aviva rather than client-specific, cost-justified assertions grounded in evidence. The discrepancy between the claim of lower costs and the arithmetic showing the existing Standard Life at 0.18% is lower than the recommended 0.44%, combined with the internal text contradiction on page 6, means the core benefit claim—lower costs—is not supported by and is contradicted by the evidence provided.","citations":[{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existin