nts and lump sum payments you take.' (3) Death benefits under age 75 are tax-free; after age 75 taxed at beneficiary's marginal rate, matching the report's statement. (4) References to lump sum allowance (£268,275) and lump sum & death benefit allowance (£1,073,100) are detailed in product literature (P2, P4, P6). (5) The small pots rule explanation in the report on page 6 matches the generic disclosure structure but the specific net receipt estimate of £170 is an adviser calculation not independently verified in the evidence. (6) The Flexi Access Drawdown section (P6) confirms the mechanism but does not personalise it to this client's circumstances.","discrepancies":["The report provides a general tax caveat and generic pension freedom explanations but does not establish the client's personal marginal tax rate, which is essential to quantify the tax consequence of income drawdown withdrawals under FAD.","For the Scottish Widows encashment small pot calculation (£170 net from £200 withdrawal), the report does not state the client's marginal tax rate or how £170 was derived from the 25% tax-free / 75% taxable split, making it impossible to verify the calculation against the client's actual tax position.","The report states on page 9 that the client 'prefer[s] the flexibility of entering Flexi Access Drawdown' but does not explain the tax consequence of regular income withdrawals under FAD at the client's marginal rate, which is material to understanding the retirement income strategy.","Death benefit taxation is explained generically (pre-75 tax-free, post-75 at beneficiary's rate) but the client is aged 69, so the pre-75 scenario is highly material and time-limited; the report does not emphasise this urgency or its implication for planning.","The report mentions tax relief on contributions (£80 net = £100 gross) as a general principle but does not address whether the client, as a higher earner or depending on income band, receives basic rate (20%) or higher rate (40%) relief, leaving a material tax consequence unquantified.","The report does not disclose the HMRC lump sum allowance (£268,275) or lump sum & death benefit allowance (£1,073,100) limits to the client, though these are referenced in product literature (P2, P4, P6); these are material constraints on tax-free benefits."],"comparisonPerformed":false,"missingInputs":["Client's personal marginal tax rate (essential to quantify income tax on FAD withdrawals and small pot encashment).","Confirmation of client's employment income band to establish whether tax relief on contributions is at basic rate (20%) or higher rate (40%).","Derivation and verification of the £170 net receipt calculation for the Scottish Widows £200 small pot encashment.","Client's awareness of and consent to the HMRC lump sum allowance and lump sum & death benefit allowance limits.","Any other pension benefits or arrangements outside this suitability report that might affect the client's use of allowances."],"analysis":"The report states multiple tax consequences but the comparison cannot be fully performed because the client's personal tax circumstances—specifically marginal tax rate—are not established in the canonical model or evidence, yet they are material to quantifying the actual tax impact of the recommendation. The generic tax statements (caveat on page 16, death benefits rules, pension freedoms overview) are corroborated by product literature (P2, P4, P6) and meet the standard disclosure requirement. However, a tax consequence becomes material and requires specific disclosure when it directly flows from the recommendation and affects the client's net benefit. For the small pots encashment, the report provides a specific estimate (£170 net from £200) but does not show the calculation or state the assumed tax rate, making it unverifiable. The report's reference to entering Flexi Access Drawdown is presented as the client's preference but the material tax consequence—that each withdrawal is taxed at the client's marginal rate, with no upper limit—is stated only in generic form on page 9 and page 6, not in relation to the client's specific retirement income needs or tax position. The age 75 death benefit threshold is material (client is 69) but not emphasised as a time-critical planning point. The lump sum and death benefit allowances are disclosed in product literature but not brought to the client's attention in the suitability report itself, creating a gap between what the file knows and what the report tells the client. The guard 'A tax consequence stated without the client's tax band established is unverifiable, not necessarily wrong' applies: the small pot calculation cannot be verified without knowing the assumed marginal rate. The guard 'Generic legislative explanation is not the same as stating the consequence for this client' also applies: while pension freedoms and death benefit rules are explained generically and correctly, their specific impact on this client's retirement income, tax position and estate planning is not articulated.","citations":[{"passageId":"P7","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"All statements concerning the tax treatment of products and their benefits are based on our understanding of current tax law and HMRC practice. Levels and basis of tax relief are subject to change.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Flexi-access drawdown provides a flexible and tax-efficient means of drawing benefits from a pension fund. An individual can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275 for people with no form of lifetime allowance protection). The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment from which the individual can take regular or ad-hoc income withdrawals to suit their needs and circumstances. There is no upper limit to the